1. e4 {Notes by Morphy} e6 2. f4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. c3
f6 6. Na3 Nh6 7. Nc2 Nf7 8. d4 Qb6 9. Ne3 {Premature. He
should have moved 9.Bd3. The great fault to be found with
Mcdonnell's play in all these Sicilian and French openings, as
also in the Queen's Gambits, is loss of time, which, against
such a powerful antagonist as Labourdonnais, could ill be
afforded by any player. } cxd4 10. cxd4 Bb4+ 11. Kf2 fxe5
12. fxe5 O-O 13. Kg3 Qc7 14. h4 Nfxe5 15. dxe5 Nxe5 16. Kh3
